<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily I of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1371">1371</a>–1425</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily I was grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1389 to 1425, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>. With the khan's consent he annexed the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> in 1392, married Sophia, daughter of Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s advance in 1395 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a>'s siege of Moscow in 1408. His death left his ten-year-old son Vasily II facing a long civil war with his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-of-zvenigorod">Yury of Zvenigorod</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-i-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Vasily I of Moscow" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonimus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasily_I_and_Sofia_Vitovtovna_-_sakkos_of_Metropolitan_Photius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
